Cell["\<\
Figure 1.1  The four ENSO monitoring regions in the tropical Pacific. The Ni\
\[NTilde]o 3.4 box, highlighted, is the standard for operational ENSO \
classification. Generated for this notebook with OpenAI gpt-image-2.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{70, 70}, {2, 14}},
 FontSize->11,
 FontSlant->Italic,
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.35],ExpressionUUID->"642dee8f-54ec-4460-b933-7e4fd9b5d06f"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Why ENSO exists.",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " Three ingredients combine into a self-sustaining oscillation:"
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"930cc37c-f18a-4cdc-8f6b-daf03f8b4d9a"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "(i) ",
 StyleBox["Bjerknes feedback",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " (Bjerknes 1969). Normally, easterly trade winds push warm surface water \
westward, piling it up in the Indonesian warm pool and tilting the \
thermocline (the boundary between warm surface water and cold deep water) \
upward in the east. A small east-Pacific warming weakens this east\[Dash]west \
SST gradient, which weakens the trades, which weakens the upwelling of cold \
water in the east, which amplifies the original warming. Positive feedback."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"2099b24e-9a52-4780-84b7-8a908a91070d"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "(ii) ",
 StyleBox["Delayed ocean dynamics",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 ". The same wind weakening that drives the warming also launches equatorial \
Kelvin waves that depress the thermocline in the east (more warming) and \
equatorial Rossby waves that reflect off the western boundary as upwelling \
Kelvin waves several months later. The reflected upwelling eventually cools \
the east and reverses the cycle."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"4f1debbb-45fa-4626-82b9-72e921f08b22"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "(iii) ",
 StyleBox["Stochastic forcing",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 ". Westerly wind bursts (WWBs) in the western Pacific \[LongDash] \
short-lived bursts driven by the Madden-Julian Oscillation, tropical \
cyclones, and other synoptic noise \[LongDash] inject impulsive disturbances \
that can push the system across thresholds. WWBs are most active in boreal \
spring (March\[Dash]April), a fact that will reappear later in this notebook \
as a key driver of the spring predictability barrier."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"32679642-3df8-4b20-acdc-507efe7a111d"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["External asset.",
  FontWeight->Bold,
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " ",
 "Figure 1.2",
 " was generated outside Wolfram Language with OpenAI's ",
 StyleBox["gpt-image-2",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " model (the prompts and a one-off Python driver are checked into ",
 StyleBox["docs/images/figures-generated/",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " of the project repository). It is included as a static illustration; there \
is no WL code to run for this figure. See \[Section]9 Acknowledgements for \
the licence."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{4, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellMargins->{{70, 70}, {6, 4}},
 CellFrameColor->RGBColor[0.45, 0.55, 0.7],
 FontSize->11,
 FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3],
 Background->RGBColor[
  0.88, 0.91, 0.95],ExpressionUUID->"67505c04-d983-4916-b3eb-2f6e9d548bf6"],

