    {
     PaneBox[
      StyleBox["\<\"Figure 3.5.1  15-month NOAA ERSSTv5 SST-anomaly \
time-lapse, February 2025 \[Rule] April 2026, overlaid at 65 % opacity on \
Wolfram's satellite basemap. The Ni\[NTilde]o 3.4 monitoring box (5\[Degree]N\
\[Dash]5\[Degree]S, 170\[Degree]W\[Dash]120\[Degree]W) is outlined in yellow; \
the basin-mean Ni\[NTilde]o 3.4 anomaly is in the upper-left badge. Colour \
scale: bright red \[Tilde] +3 \[Degree]C, deep blue \[Tilde] \[Minus]3 \
\[Degree]C, white = 0.\"\>",
       StripOnInput->False,
       FontFamily->"Helvetica",
       FontSize->10,
       FontSlant->Italic,
       FontColor->GrayLevel[0.35]],
      Alignment->Center,
      ImageSize->640]}
   },
   DefaultBaseStyle->"Column",
   GridBoxAlignment->{"Columns" -> {{Center}}},
   GridBoxItemSize->{"Columns" -> {{Automatic}}, "Rows" -> {{Automatic}}},
   GridBoxSpacings->{"Columns" -> {{Automatic}}, "Rows" -> {{0.6}}}],
  "Column"]], "Output",
 CellMargins->{{Automatic, Automatic}, {10, 14}},
 ShowCellLabel->False,
 TextAlignment->Center,ExpressionUUID->"a404ae5b-9ee7-4d47-9228-1fcdb2fb9fdb"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["What the animation shows.",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " The early frames (spring /summer 2025) sit in the tail end of the 2024\
\[Dash]25 La Ni\[NTilde]a: a cool band along the equator between the dateline \
and the South American coast, with the warm pool concentrated west of 160\
\[Degree]E. Through autumn 2025 the cold tongue weakens but stays present; \
the basin remains sub-thermal. The shift begins in December \
2025\[Dash]January 2026: the central Pacific (around 170\[Degree]W) warms \
first, then the warm signature propagates eastward along the equator through \
Feb\[Dash]Mar\[Dash]Apr 2026. By the final frame the eastern Pacific cold \
tongue has retreated to a thin sliver against the South American coast, and \
the basin-mean is +0.37 \[Degree]C. This is the spatial manifestation of the \
recharge\[Dash]discharge cycle described in \[Section]5.1 and animated in the \
toy model in Figures 5.3\[Dash]5.4."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"95d2b6b0-2ce0-47ca-9045-d62c4ba4d5ce"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Note on resolution.",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " ERSSTv5 is a 2\[Degree]\[Times]2\[Degree] gridded product designed for \
long-term climate analysis (data back to 1854). The visible blockiness of the \
overlay is the underlying grid; finer-resolution products like NOAA OISST \
(0.25\[Degree], available daily) exist but at the cost of a much shorter \
homogeneous historical record. For the present analysis the coarser ERSSTv5 \
grid suffices, and the geographic context provided by the satellite basemap \
(the visible coastlines of Asia, Australia, and the Americas) makes the \
location of the equatorial cold tongue unambiguous to a non-specialist reader."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"315bf02f-3ca7-4891-99b5-ddf16455764e"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["4.  The spring predictability barrier", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"6258868f-7752-4fd6-b7f0-8d3399b0d24b"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["The intuitive version.",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " Imagine you are a forecaster in January. The tropical Pacific is in some \
particular state (some Ni\[NTilde]o 3.4 anomaly, some thermocline tilt). Your \
job is to predict what the state will be next August. Now imagine instead \
that you are a forecaster in July of the previous year, trying to predict the \
same August. Intuitively, the January forecaster should have an easier time \
\[LongDash] she is closer in time. The shocking empirical fact about ENSO is \
that the opposite is true: ",
 StyleBox["the July forecaster, with a 13-month lead, beats the January \
forecaster, with a 7-month lead",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 ", because the January forecast has to look through the spring barrier."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"e4f2f8dd-555a-4759-bd84-120a0bba8558"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["External asset.",
  FontWeight->Bold,
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " ",
 "Figure 4.1",
 " was generated outside Wolfram Language with OpenAI's ",
 StyleBox["gpt-image-2",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " model (the prompts and a one-off Python driver are checked into ",
 StyleBox["docs/images/figures-generated/",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " of the project repository). It is included as a static illustration; there \
is no WL code to run for this figure. See \[Section]9 Acknowledgements for \
the licence."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{4, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellMargins->{{70, 70}, {6, 4}},
 CellFrameColor->RGBColor[0.45, 0.55, 0.7],
 FontSize->11,
 FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3],
 Background->RGBColor[
  0.88, 0.91, 0.95],ExpressionUUID->"0619ddeb-0d59-4165-a592-749ae052fa22"],

