Cell["\<\
Figure 5.6  Ensemble standard deviation as a function of initial month \
m\:2080 and lead. The bright vertical/diagonal band aligns with the \
verification window crossing the model's growth season (Sep\[Dash]Nov), where \
the seasonal R(t) amplifies any noise accumulated en route. The pattern is \
the toy model's signature of the same barrier physics.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{70, 70}, {2, 14}},
 FontSize->11,
 FontSlant->Italic,
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.35],ExpressionUUID->"2f1326a5-70f0-46da-911b-f7c27ece1764"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["What the model gets right and what it misses.",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " The RDO correctly reproduces (i) the broadband 3\[Dash]7-year period, (ii) \
the seasonal phase locking (events peak in DJF), (iii) the asymmetric \
ensemble cones across initial months, and (iv) the order-of-magnitude \
ensemble spread (\[Sigma] \[TildeTilde] 0.5\[Dash]1.0 \[Degree]C at 6-month \
lead) that operational dynamical models also produce. It misses (i) the El-Ni\
\[NTilde]o\[Dash]La-Ni\[NTilde]a amplitude asymmetry that requires the \
nonlinear cubic damping term in Jin's full equations, and (ii) the \
extratropical influences on the ENSO state that more complete models \
include."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"525c1bc3-369a-4fe3-b448-7035c1d3ec73"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["5.2  Delayed-action oscillator (Suarez & Schopf 1988)", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"f824153b-e19f-43b9-b070-d4157ca6c49b"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["A different conceptual reduction.",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " Suarez & Schopf (1988) take the same physics and reduce it to a single \
scalar SST anomaly with an explicit time delay. The delay represents the \
basin-crossing time of equatorial Rossby + Kelvin waves: an SST warming \
forces a wind stress that excites a Rossby wave westward; the wave reflects \
off the western boundary as an upwelling Kelvin wave; the Kelvin wave returns \
east and cools the SST. The round-trip time is \[Delta] \[TildeTilde] 6 \
months."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"9dcdb297-78c2-45a1-ba7a-f4c037b60b12"],

Cell[BoxData[
 FormBox[
  RowBox[{
   FractionBox["dT", "dt"], "=", "T", "-", 
   RowBox[{"\[Alpha]", " ", 
    RowBox[{"T", "(", 
     RowBox[{"t", "-", "\[Delta]"}], ")"}]}], "-", 
   SuperscriptBox["T", "3"], "+", 
   RowBox[{"\[Sigma]", "(", "t", ")", " ", "\[Xi]", "(", "t", ")"}]}], 
  TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormula",
 CellMargins->{{80, 50}, {8, 
  8}},ExpressionUUID->"c046d531-9f2b-4571-88a5-9fa4e6819353"],

Cell["\<\
The +T term is the Bjerknes feedback (growth); the negative \[Alpha] T(t-\
\[Delta]) term is the delayed wave return (negative feedback); the \[Minus]T\
\.b3 term is the nonlinear damping that limits the amplitude; and \[Sigma](t) \
\[Xi](t) is again seasonally modulated stochastic WWB forcing. Time is \
rescaled so that one model time-unit = two calendar months, putting the \
canonical 6-month wave delay at \[Delta] = 3 in dimensionless units.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"1d052598-39a9-4793-867e-a1ed76004f86"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Bifurcation structure.",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " There are two distinct linearisations to keep track of: stability of the \
trivial fixed point ",
 StyleBox["T = 0",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 ", and stability of the nonzero fixed points ",
 StyleBox["T\.b2 = \[PlusMinus]\[Sqrt](1\[Minus]\[Alpha])",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " that emerge when the trivial one loses stability."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"7e9261ee-bc5e-482d-b48a-dd1f4b888095"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["(a) Linearisation about ",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 StyleBox["T = 0",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 StyleBox[".",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " Substituting ",
 StyleBox["T \[Tilde] e^(\[Lambda] t)",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " gives the characteristic equation ",
 StyleBox["\[Lambda] = 1 \[Minus] \[Alpha] e^(\[Minus] \[Lambda] \[Delta])",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 ". This is transcendental and admits infinitely many complex eigenvalues. At \
",
 StyleBox["\[Alpha] = 1",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " the real part of the leading (real) eigenvalue passes through zero \
\[LongDash] this is the pitchfork transition, marking the loss of stability \
of the trivial fixed point."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"4e6872fc-d490-4ddc-a236-4113792fa699"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["(b) Linearisation about ",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 StyleBox["T\.b2 = \[PlusMinus]\[Sqrt](1\[Minus]\[Alpha])",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 StyleBox[".",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " For ",
 StyleBox["\[Alpha] < 1",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " the origin is unstable and the cubic damping ",
 StyleBox["\[Minus] T^3",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " saturates trajectories near these nonzero fixed points. Their magnitude ",
 StyleBox["decreases",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " with ",
 StyleBox["\[Alpha]",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " (from \[PlusMinus]1 at ",
 StyleBox["\[Alpha] = 0",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " down to 0 at the pitchfork). A parallel linearisation around ",
 StyleBox["T\.b2",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " yields its own characteristic equation; for small ",
 StyleBox["\[Alpha]",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " or small ",
 StyleBox["\[Delta]",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " the nonzero fixed points are stable and the trajectory rattles within one \
basin, while above a critical line in the ",
 StyleBox["(\[Alpha], \[Delta])",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " plane a delay-induced Hopf bifurcation destabilises ",
 StyleBox["T\.b2",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " and drives a sustained limit cycle of period ",
 StyleBox["\[TildeTilde] 4\[Delta]",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " (i.e. 24 model months \[LongDash] roughly 4 years in calendar time at our \
rescaling)."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"b56f0099-5b50-4eea-ae68-2d1586b6ac28"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Dynamic amplitude vs deterministic fixed point.",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " These two analyses pull in opposite directions on the stationary \
peak-to-peak observed in the stochastic runs. At small ",
 StyleBox["\[Alpha]",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " the trajectory sits in one ",
 StyleBox["T\.b2",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " basin and the peak-to-peak swing is whatever the noise rattles \[LongDash] \
empirically about 0.9 \[Degree]C for our noise calibration (read from the \
bifurcation sweep below; ",
 StyleBox["not",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " derivable from ",
 StyleBox["\[Sqrt](1\[Minus]\[Alpha])",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " alone, which only gives the fixed-point ",
 StyleBox["separation",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 "). At larger ",
 StyleBox["\[Alpha]",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " the delayed negative feedback flips the sign each half-period and the \
dynamic amplitude grows to a full peak-to-peak ",
 StyleBox["\[TildeTilde] 3",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " (i.e. ",
 StyleBox["\[PlusMinus]1.5",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 "). Below we sweep ",
 StyleBox["\[Alpha]",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " at fixed ",
 StyleBox["\[Delta] = 3",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " and plot the stationary min/max envelope:"
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"89b0d5e1-4c08-4c7b-98e4-e6d960f1ca78"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
(* Figure 5.7: bifurcation sweep -- min/max of T as a function of
   the wave-coupling alpha, at fixed delta = 3.  simulateDDE[] (shown
   in full below) is the same Euler-Maruyama DDE integrator used for
   the other delayed-osc panels; full implementation in
   the helpers package (loaded by the Setup cell). *)
alphaList = Range[0.3, 1.15, 0.05];
bifurc = Table[
   Block[{alphaDel = aL},
      With[{r = simulateDDE[1, 600., 0.1, 0.3, 13]},
         {aL, MinMax @ Drop[r[\"T\"], Round[200./0.1]]}]],
   {aL, alphaList}];
Show[
   ListPlot[{#1, #2[[1]]} & @@@ bifurc,
      PlotStyle -> Directive[PointSize[0.012], RGBColor[0.20, 0.45, 0.75]]],
   ListPlot[{#1, #2[[2]]} & @@@ bifurc,
      PlotStyle -> Directive[PointSize[0.012], RGBColor[0.85, 0.32, 0.10]]],
   Frame -> True,
   FrameLabel -> {{\"min / max of T (steady state)\", None},
                  {\"wave coupling \\[Alpha]\", None}},
   GridLines -> {{0.5, 1.0}, {0}},
   ImageSize -> 720, AspectRatio -> 0.55]\
\>", "Input",
 CellMargins->{{60, 30}, {6, 6}},
 FontSize->11,ExpressionUUID->"e19fe2b8-ce94-4033-9620-293ffb53c05b"],

