Cell["\<\
Figure 6.2  Three-panel live overlay: IRI/CPC operational plume (top), \
toy-model ensemble cone (middle), official NOAA probability bars (bottom). \
All three panels share the same calendar horizon.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{70, 70}, {2, 14}},
 FontSize->11,
 FontSlant->Italic,
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.35],ExpressionUUID->"5f582ad4-84d1-4988-8841-00cb7f5bffda"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Toy-model probability of El Ni\[NTilde]o vs the NOAA issuance.",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " The fraction of the ensemble (500-member in the pre-rendered figure, 200 \
in the live wlIn cell) whose monthly T exceeds the +0.5 \[Degree]C threshold, \
season by season, gives the toy model's analogue of the NOAA probability \
bar:"
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"55993aca-8be7-4d03-8b84-4d6935a07f5a"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Estimated thermocline state from the observed monthly tendency: ",
 StyleBox["4.88",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " (large positive recharge consistent with the residual heat content from \
the previous La Ni\[NTilde]a). The toy-model ensemble is initialised from (T, \
h) = (",
 StyleBox["0.23",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 ", ",
 StyleBox["4.88",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 "). ",
 StyleBox[" Note: the values above were computed at notebook-build time; \
re-evaluate the wlIn cell at the start of \[Section]6 (which calls \
LoadNino34[]) to refresh against today's NOAA PSL data.",
  FontSlant->Italic]
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"e20488c3-5ad8-485e-96d7-4fa5fb9aada3"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Caveats.",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " The toy model is, well, a toy. Operational forecast systems use full 3-D \
coupled GCMs with assimilated subsurface ocean temperature, satellite wind \
stress, and tens of thousands of ensemble members. Our 2-variable system \
necessarily misses (i) the spatial structure of the equatorial Pacific \
(different events have different east-west patterns: \"central Pacific\" El \
Ni\[NTilde]o vs \"east Pacific\" El Ni\[NTilde]o), (ii) interactions with the \
extratropical atmosphere (PDO, NAO), and (iii) any climate-change-driven \
trends in either the mean state or the variability. The cone should therefore \
be interpreted as a sanity-check on the operational forecast \[LongDash] not \
as competition with it."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"ebf31b7f-dcd6-4028-bac0-b3ed4e53f709"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["7.  Conclusions and outlook", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"405d0260-7629-48fa-836f-02f39d83b850"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["What we showed.",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " (1) The May 2026 El Ni\[NTilde]o Watch is supported by a clear emergence \
signal in the observational record: a Feb\[Dash]Apr Ni\[NTilde]o 3.4 warming \
in the top decile of all such windows since 1950 (ranked 9th of 77), combined \
with substantial residual subsurface heat content from the previous La Ni\
\[NTilde]a recharge. (2) The spring predictability barrier is unambiguous in \
77 years of observations: forecasts initialised in boreal winter collapse to \
near-zero skill by mid-summer, while forecasts initialised in late summer \
keep skill all the way to the following spring. (3) Both classical ENSO toy \
models (recharge\[Dash]discharge and delayed-action) can be implemented in \
under 100 lines of Wolfram Language, and the recharge\[Dash]discharge model \
with seasonally varying Bjerknes feedback and seasonally varying WWB forcing \
reproduces the spring-barrier mechanism quantitatively. (4) Initialising the \
toy model from the current observed state produces a live ensemble forecast \
cone consistent with the NOAA 96 % peak-DJF El Ni\[NTilde]o probability."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"9703475c-aeb8-4876-ac9b-884de5287097"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["What to watch in the next 60 days.",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " The next NOAA ENSO Diagnostic Discussion is issued in mid-June 2026. Three \
numbers will tell us whether the developing event is on track:"
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"9e880e11-17a0-4405-9055-3b31a811909f"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "\[Bullet] ",
 StyleBox["May 2026 Ni\[NTilde]o 3.4 monthly anomaly",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 ". The model predicts +0.5 to +1.0 \[Degree]C (median +0.7); if the observed \
value is at the high end, expect the next discussion to issue an El Ni\
\[NTilde]o ",
 StyleBox["Advisory",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " (not just a Watch)."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"01d3c3a8-3380-4e4e-a60d-baf66ac5af74"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "\[Bullet] ",
 StyleBox["MAM 2026 ONI",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " (March\[Dash]April\[Dash]May three-month running mean). The +0.5 \
\[Degree]C threshold for one of the five-consecutive-season El-Ni\[NTilde]o \
criterion needs to be crossed for the first time. The model gives 0.30 \
\[Degree]C (5\[Dash]95 % CI: 0.00 to 0.65)."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"a7c65f2f-1d77-4fa2-a9ff-14b67739db56"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "\[Bullet] ",
 StyleBox["Westerly wind burst activity",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " through mid-June, watched in real time on the TAO mooring array. A strong \
late-spring WWB would push the central Pacific thermocline anomaly further \
east and accelerate the warming."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"d10978b0-f0c7-468b-afff-15eba76ca626"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["What the barrier means for the forecast users.",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " The operational ENSO probability bars from May 2026 give 96 % for DJF. \
This number sounds confident but should be read with the barrier in mind: it \
summarises model agreement after the barrier window has already been crossed \
(model initial conditions were set in mid-April; forecasts shoot forward from \
there). Confidence in events further out should be qualified \[LongDash] \
e.g., the DJF probability is robust because the system is past the barrier, \
but the JFM 2027 \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]event-decay\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] \
details will look quite different by July."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"8be2b62c-cec3-4fc5-aa2c-dc2bfd0f3867"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

